Q: How to change the background color of a TButton component with code in Delphi FMX? A: <p>Changing the background color of a plain `TButton` at runtime in FMX is tricky because the default styles (especially on Windows) often use bitmaps/`TSubImage` instead of a simple `TRectangle` for the background. That's why your `FindStyleResource('background') as TRectangle` check fails, it's usually not a `TRectangle`.</p>
<p>The quickest reliable way without creating custom styles for every button (or replacing them with `TRectangle`s) is to overlay a new `TRectangle` on top of the background resource at runtime. It sits behind the text/glyph and gives you a solid color fill.</p>
<p>Here's a helper procedure you can drop in a unit or your form:</p>
<pre><code>procedure SetButtonColor(AButton: TButton; AColor: TAlphaColor);
var
  Bg: TFmxObject;
  Rect: TRectangle;
begin
  if AButton = nil then Exit;

  // Make sure the style is loaded
  AButton.NeedStyleLookup;
  AButton.ApplyStyleLookup;

  Bg := AButton.FindStyleResource('background');
  if Bg = nil then
    Bg := AButton.FindStyleResource('button');  // fallback for some styles

  if Bg &lt;&gt; nil then
  begin
    // Optional: remove old overlay rectangles if you call this multiple times
    for var I := Bg.ChildrenCount - 1 downto 0 do
      if Bg.Children[I] is TRectangle then
        Bg.Children[I].Free;

    Rect := TRectangle.Create(Bg);
    Rect.Parent := TControl(Bg);
    Rect.Align := TAlignLayout.Client;
    Rect.Fill.Color := AColor;
    Rect.Fill.Kind := TBrushKind.Solid;
    Rect.Stroke.Kind := TBrushKind.None;
    Rect.HitTest := False;
    Rect.SendToBack;  // important so text stays on top
    Rect.Visible := True;
  end;

  AButton.Repaint;
end;
</code></pre>
<p>How to use it:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>// e.g. in FormCreate, after loading DB colors
SetButtonColor(Button1, TAlphaColorRec.Red);
SetButtonColor(Button2, $FF00FF00);  // lime, or whatever from DB
</code></pre>
<p>This should work cross-platform and doesn't mess with the existing style too much. The pressed/hover effects from the original style usually still apply on top.</p>

Q: Why does this require a const_cast? A: <p>In short, to answer your first quesiton, yes, the <code>const</code> ness is essentially imbued to member variables (see <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/language/const.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>, in the structs example). The whole object is considered const. If you don't want this to be the case, you can mark one of your members as <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/cv.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mutable</a>.</p>
<p>Your second question is answered by the link above. Since the member variable is actually considered const, <code>const_cast</code>ing away its constness is undefined behavior, in the same way that this is:</p>
<pre><code>const int i = 1;
const int&amp; this_guy = i;
const_cast&lt;int&amp;&gt;(this_guy) = 2; // UB
</code></pre>

Q: java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper A: <ol>
<li><p>Yes, this is a <strong>dependency conflict</strong>, made more visible after upgrading Spring.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Spring 5 expects Jackson 2.x (<code>com.fasterxml.jackson.databind</code>) and drops all compatibility with Jackson 1.x.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Your project must <strong>explicitly include</strong> Jackson 2.x and <strong>stop using Jackson 1.x</strong> to avoid runtime issues.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>In your pom.xml, add:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;com.fasterxml.jackson.core&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;jackson-databind&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;2.11.0&lt;/version&gt; &lt;!-- or newer, as needed --&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Also, include:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;com.fasterxml.jackson.core&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;jackson-core&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;2.11.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;com.fasterxml.jackson.core&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;jackson-annotations&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;2.11.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>
